PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Round 0: I initially leaned B (hemolymph-secreted Dilp2), though E was a credible cautious alternative. Public responses were split, with B and E dominant and one D. Key evidence for B: fat-body Imp-L2 “soaks up” hemolymph Dilp2 and blocks NSC reactivation, and Dilp2 can cross the BBB, so circulating IPC-derived Dilp2 can reach the brain. Key evidence for caution/E: Imp-L2 itself crosses the BBB, so fat-body Imp-L2 may also neutralize central Dilp2; glial Imp-L2 is confounded because it also reduces IPC Dilp2 expression; DRN inhibition only delays reactivation and DRNs may act independently of Dilp2 absorption; high bovine insulin only shows NSCs can respond to an insulin-like signal.

Round 1: Public split remains essentially 5 B vs 5 E, with no A or D. B supporters (Colleagues 1, 6, 7, 8, 10) argue the fat-body Imp-L2 experiment most directly implicates the hemolymph pool and that DRN involvement is weak/independent. E supporters (Colleagues 2, 3, 4, 5, 9) argue the fat-body experiment is not clean because Imp-L2 crosses the BBB and could act centrally, and glial Imp-L2 is confounded by reduced IPC Dilp2 expression. They also stress that DRN inhibition only delays and bovine insulin is non-physiological.

My current assessment: I still lean toward B as the best-supported intended answer, because the fat-body Imp-L2 manipulation specifically targets the hemolymph Dilp2 pool and the DRN route is not established as the source; DRNs may be modulatory/independent. However, confidence is only moderate. The strongest unresolved issue is whether fat-body-derived Imp-L2 acts centrally after crossing the BBB, which would prevent cleanly distinguishing hemolymph-secreted Dilp2 from DRN-transported/central Dilp2. If strict causal isolation is required, E remains defensible. I will keep watching for any evidence that isolates hemolymph vs central Dilp2 or shows DRN-derived Dilp2 is necessary/sufficient.}

